WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.200
Welcome back to Building
Ticket-to-Machine.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.113
This is Chapter 3: the
SailPoint client and its scopes.

3
00:00:06.113 --> 00:00:09.295
An identity governance
API client that can read

4
00:00:09.295 --> 00:00:12.399
every identity is already
a sensitive account.

5
00:00:12.399 --> 00:00:16.313
One that can also change them is a
second administrator nobody named.

6
00:00:16.313 --> 00:00:21.714
This chapter shows how the kit's clients
reach SailPoint Identity Security Cloud and

7
00:00:21.714 --> 00:00:24.757
Microsoft Graph, adds their
rows to the call list,

8
00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:28.637
and proves the drift and re-read
clients hold read scopes only.

9
00:00:28.637 --> 00:00:31.360
Then two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:36.822
Why insist on read scopes?

11
00:00:36.822 --> 00:00:38.760
Look at what the clients actually do.

12
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.959
The drift client reads identities,

13
00:00:40.959 --> 00:00:45.735
uncorrelated accounts and Graph users,
and optionally the directory audit.

14
00:00:45.735 --> 00:00:50.272
The re-read client reads one
account, one identity or one user.

15
00:00:50.272 --> 00:00:52.711
Nothing in Pass 1 writes anywhere.

16
00:00:52.711 --> 00:00:56.714
So a manage scope on either client
buys nothing and risks a lot:

17
00:00:56.714 --> 00:00:59.148
a stolen token could change accounts.

18
00:00:59.148 --> 00:01:02.070
The workflow scope left
on a standing client lets

19
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.922
anyone add workflows that
call out of SailPoint.

20
00:01:04.922 --> 00:01:09.780
And an administrator's personal token
runs the whole kit as an org admin.

21
00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.943
Here's what the shared
client already does.

22
00:01:15.943 --> 00:01:20.592
It builds SailPoint's base URL from
the tenant name, or takes one you set.

23
00:01:20.592 --> 00:01:23.982
It gets a token from a vault
or by client credentials;

24
00:01:23.982 --> 00:01:26.742
a personal access token
works the same way.

25
00:01:26.742 --> 00:01:29.831
Search pages with
search-after, sorted on the ID,

26
00:01:29.831 --> 00:01:33.792
which is how SailPoint says to
get past ten thousand results.

27
00:01:33.792 --> 00:01:38.579
Account lists page two hundred
fifty at a time, the API's maximum.

28
00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:42.530
Graph uses a certificate first,
from the cloud's own authority.

29
00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.156
And because Search has no accounts index,

30
00:01:45.156 --> 00:01:48.530
one account is re-read
through the Accounts API.

31
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.318
These are the rows this chapter adds.

32
00:01:54.318 --> 00:01:59.231
On SailPoint, a token from an API client,
then Search with the search read scope,

33
00:01:59.231 --> 00:02:03.992
and the Accounts list and single-account
read with the accounts read scope.

34
00:02:03.992 --> 00:02:07.941
Workflow creation needs a manage
scope, and it's its own task,

35
00:02:07.941 --> 00:02:10.842
because no client in
the kit makes that call.

36
00:02:10.842 --> 00:02:15.895
On Graph, listing users and reading one
user both need User dot Read dot All as an

37
00:02:15.895 --> 00:02:19.081
application permission,
and the directory audit,

38
00:02:19.081 --> 00:02:22.630
which is optional, needs
AuditLog dot Read dot All.

39
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.768
Four choices make the rows enforceable.

40
00:02:28.768 --> 00:02:32.716
The Accounts endpoints accept
either a read or a manage scope;

41
00:02:32.716 --> 00:02:37.206
the list names the read scope, and
the comparison flags manage as extra.

42
00:02:37.206 --> 00:02:39.938
Workflow creation gets a separate client,

43
00:02:39.938 --> 00:02:44.155
used once and then removed, which
is the program's design guidance.

44
00:02:44.155 --> 00:02:46.569
The comparison works for SailPoint scopes

45
00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:49.118
and for Graph application
permissions alike.

46
00:02:49.118 --> 00:02:54.046
And a required-only option leaves out optional
calls, so an app registration without

47
00:02:54.046 --> 00:02:58.840
the audit permission still passes for a
drift run that doesn't read the audit.

48
00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:04.254
No new test file this time:

49
00:03:04.254 --> 00:03:07.978
Chapter 2's call-list test
covers SailPoint and Graph too.

50
00:03:07.978 --> 00:03:11.508
The drift and re-read clients
run against a fake session,

51
00:03:11.508 --> 00:03:15.191
and every request they make must
match a row for their task.

52
00:03:15.191 --> 00:03:19.139
It also asserts that no task asks
for a scope ending in manage,

53
00:03:19.139 --> 00:03:24.115
or a Graph permission ending in ReadWrite
dot All, except workflow creation.

54
00:03:24.115 --> 00:03:27.236
And the kit's original
SailPoint tests still pass:

55
00:03:27.236 --> 00:03:32.100
the token by client credentials and
from a vault, and both paging styles.

56
00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:39.400
The SailPoint facts, each
read on November first.

57
00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.639
The token endpoint supports
client credentials

58
00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:45.562
and personal access
tokens, up to ten per user.

59
00:03:45.562 --> 00:03:48.848
A client-credentials
token has no user context,

60
00:03:48.848 --> 00:03:53.987
and SailPoint warns that most admin
APIs often return forbidden with one.

61
00:03:53.987 --> 00:03:58.876
Search needs the search read scope and
pages past ten thousand with search-after.

62
00:03:58.876 --> 00:04:02.300
Its indexes include
identities but no accounts.

63
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.984
The Accounts list and get
accept a read or manage scope,

64
00:04:05.984 --> 00:04:08.112
at most two hundred fifty a page.

65
00:04:08.112 --> 00:04:11.616
And creating a workflow needs
the workflow manage scope,

66
00:04:11.616 --> 00:04:16.900
and on a November ninth re-read, both
workflow calls name the org admin user level.

67
00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:22.063
And the Graph facts.

68
00:04:22.063 --> 00:04:27.285
User dot Read dot All is the least-privileged
application permission both to list

69
00:04:27.285 --> 00:04:31.432
users and to read one, and the audit
needs AuditLog dot Read dot All;

70
00:04:31.432 --> 00:04:34.812
both reads are available in
the US Government clouds.

71
00:04:34.812 --> 00:04:39.522
One wrinkle: application permissions
aren't supported on the manager endpoint,

72
00:04:39.522 --> 00:04:43.588
and Learn documents expanding the
manager on the users list instead.

73
00:04:43.588 --> 00:04:47.657
Microsoft 365 GCC uses
the worldwide Graph host;

74
00:04:47.657 --> 00:04:52.438
GCC High and the Defense
Department cloud have their own.

75
00:04:52.438 --> 00:04:56.560
SailPoint's Identity Security
Cloud is FedRAMP Moderate.

76
00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:01.685
Four points are still open.

77
00:05:01.685 --> 00:05:06.960
SailPoint's public pages don't say
what API domain a FedRAMP tenant uses.

78
00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.891
They don't say whether a
client-credentials token gets

79
00:05:09.891 --> 00:05:12.822
data from Search and Accounts
or a forbidden response.

80
00:05:12.822 --> 00:05:15.137
Learn doesn't settle whether expanding the

81
00:05:15.137 --> 00:05:17.710
manager works under an
application permission.

82
00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:22.117
And feature parity in SailPoint's
FedRAMP environment, for Search,

83
00:05:22.117 --> 00:05:27.085
Workflows and the Accounts API, is a
question to put to SailPoint in writing.

84
00:05:27.085 --> 00:05:29.940
Each one is on the kit's verify list.

85
00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:38.217
What this proves: the drift and
re-read clients hold read scopes only,

86
00:05:38.217 --> 00:05:42.502
and the workflow client holds one
scope and is removed after use.

87
00:05:42.502 --> 00:05:46.058
That's least privilege and
account management at Moderate,

88
00:05:46.058 --> 00:05:49.540
with the comparison kept beside
the API client's approval.

89
00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:52.977
The Graph app registration
holds only what it reads,

90
00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:57.053
with a certificate, which adds
authenticator management, IA-5.

91
00:05:57.053 --> 00:06:01.558
And no part of Pass 1 can write to
SailPoint, Entra ID or Active Directory,

92
00:06:01.558 --> 00:06:05.147
which is the Federal
Modernization Workbook's rule that

93
00:06:05.147 --> 00:06:09.320
the coordination layer never
writes configuration itself.

94
00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.108
Who owns this?

95
00:06:14.108 --> 00:06:20.031
Developers who own the clients, the identity
engineers who own the SailPoint side,

96
00:06:20.031 --> 00:06:22.732
and the I S S O who approves API clients.

97
00:06:22.732 --> 00:06:25.108
There are three non-person
identities here.

98
00:06:25.108 --> 00:06:28.970
A standing SailPoint reader
with exactly two read scopes.

99
00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:31.455
A workflow client with one manage scope,

100
00:06:31.455 --> 00:06:34.995
used once to create the three
workflows and then removed.

101
00:06:34.995 --> 00:06:38.564
And a Graph app registration
with the user read permission,

102
00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:43.800
plus the audit permission only if you read
the audit, signed in with a certificate.

103
00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.450
Lab 1 runs on a laptop.

104
00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:52.888
First, the kit's four
SailPoint client tests pass.

105
00:06:52.888 --> 00:06:57.175
Then list what the drift client needs
from SailPoint: a token, Search with

106
00:06:57.175 --> 00:07:01.463
the search read scope, and the Accounts
list with the accounts read scope.

107
00:07:01.463 --> 00:07:02.763
That's two scopes.

108
00:07:02.763 --> 00:07:05.700
Now give it a manage
scope as well and compare.

109
00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.546
Nothing is missing, the
manage scope is extra,

110
00:07:08.546 --> 00:07:12.700
least privilege doesn't hold, and
the command exits with a failure.

111
00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:16.890
That one line is the
review an I S S O needs.

112
00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.078
Next, check the reader's scopes
against the workflow task.

113
00:07:24.078 --> 00:07:27.852
The workflow scope is missing
and both read scopes are extra:

114
00:07:27.852 --> 00:07:31.552
the comparison fails in both
directions, which is the point.

115
00:07:31.552 --> 00:07:33.453
Two jobs need two clients.

116
00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:35.353
Then the Graph app registration.

117
00:07:35.353 --> 00:07:40.104
Holding only the user read permission, it
fails a drift run that reads the audit,

118
00:07:40.104 --> 00:07:42.589
because the audit permission is missing.

119
00:07:42.589 --> 00:07:44.465
With the required-only option,

120
00:07:44.465 --> 00:07:48.730
the same grant passes for a drift
run that doesn't read the audit.

121
00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:54.999
Lab 2 is in a SailPoint sandbox tenant,

122
00:07:54.999 --> 00:07:58.168
never production, and the
authors haven't run it.

123
00:07:58.168 --> 00:08:03.654
Find the tenant's API base from its own
O auth info page rather than guessing.

124
00:08:03.654 --> 00:08:07.230
Create the two API
clients and compare each.

125
00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:09.455
Try the reader against the sandbox.

126
00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:14.409
If Search or Accounts refuses a
client-credentials token, use a personal token

127
00:08:14.409 --> 00:08:18.905
for a dedicated user at the lowest
level that works, and record which one.

128
00:08:18.905 --> 00:08:21.793
Find where the HR key
lives on an identity.

129
00:08:21.793 --> 00:08:24.750
Then delete both clients and the output.

130
00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:29.525
The dangers.

131
00:08:29.525 --> 00:08:32.656
A manage scope on a read
client means a stolen token

132
00:08:32.656 --> 00:08:35.637
can change accounts, and
the comparison flags it.

133
00:08:35.637 --> 00:08:39.925
The workflow scope left on a standing
client lets anyone add workflows

134
00:08:39.925 --> 00:08:43.413
that call out of SailPoint, so
it lives on a client used once.

135
00:08:43.413 --> 00:08:47.581
An administrator's personal token
runs the kit as an org admin,

136
00:08:47.581 --> 00:08:49.788
so use a dedicated low-level user.

137
00:08:49.788 --> 00:08:53.726
Offset paging past ten thousand
results stops silently,

138
00:08:53.726 --> 00:08:55.737
so the code uses search-after.

139
00:08:55.737 --> 00:08:58.026
The wrong Graph cloud is refused.

140
00:08:58.026 --> 00:09:02.090
And Graph signs in with a
certificate first, not a secret.

141
00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:07.040
Done looks like this.

142
00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:10.590
The SailPoint reader holds
exactly its two read scopes.

143
00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:15.415
No standing client holds the workflow
scope, and the workflow client is gone.

144
00:09:15.415 --> 00:09:18.234
Any personal token belongs to a dedicated

145
00:09:18.234 --> 00:09:21.215
user at the lowest level
that works, recorded.

146
00:09:21.215 --> 00:09:24.990
The Graph app holds only what
it reads, with a certificate.

147
00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.815
The API base came from the tenant itself.

148
00:09:27.815 --> 00:09:31.627
And the call-list test passes,
including the no-write assertion.

149
00:09:31.627 --> 00:09:34.527
Next, Chapter 4 turns a SailPoint event

150
00:09:34.527 --> 00:09:37.789
into a typed task and
sees the exact payload.

151
00:09:37.789 --> 00:09:38.960
Thanks for watching.
